{"tool_name":"pyliger","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"pyliger","home":"https://github.com/welch-lab/pyliger","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Scalable single-cell multi-omic data integration in Python.","addition_date":"2022-05-17T20:30:59.536045Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T20:09:19.661149Z","tool_type":["Library"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:pyliger","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"welchjd@umich.edu","name":"Joshua D. Welch","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5869-2391","typeEntity":"Person"},{"name":"Lu Lu"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Gene-set enrichment analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2436"}]}],"name":"PyLiger","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Jennifer","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btac190","metadata":{"abstract":"Motivation: LIGER (Linked Inference of Genomic Experimental Relationships) is a widely used R package for single-cell multi-omic data integration. However, many users prefer to analyze their single-cell datasets in Python, which offers an attractive syntax and highly optimized scientific computing libraries for increased efficiency. Results: We developed PyLiger, a Python package for integrating single-cell multi-omic datasets. PyLiger offers faster performance than the previous R implementation (2-5\u00d7 speedup), interoperability with AnnData format, flexible on-disk or in-memory analysis capability and new functionality for gene ontology enrichment analysis. The on-disk capability enables analysis of arbitrarily large single-cell datasets using fixed memory.","authors":[{"name":"Lu L."},{"name":"Welch J.D."}],"citationCount":7,"date":"2022-05-15T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"PyLiger: Scalable single-cell multi-omic data integration in Python"},"pmcid":"PMC9306758","pmid":"35561174"}],"topic":[{"term":"Data integration and warehousing",